---
// Home: the project as a short story in three chapters (plan §6, decided 2026-11-26).
// No study table and no charts here; the evidence lives on /studies/ and on each study page.
// Every number below is quoted from a closed study's study.yaml; S1 is still running, so it has none.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import { people, publishedPosts, studyPath } from "../lib/data";

const supervisor = people().find((p) => p.role === "supervisor")?.name ?? "our supervisor";
const all = await getCollection("studies");
const link = (id: string) => {
  const s = all.find((x) => x.data.id === id);
  return s ? studyPath(s) : "/studies/";
};
const hasNotes = (await publishedPosts()).length > 0;
---
<Base title="NeuroPulse" description="Four students testing whether a network can learn new tasks without forgetting, by keeping the parts it needs to function.">
  <div class="container">
    <header class="story-head">
      <h1>Can a network learn new tasks without forgetting, by keeping what it needs to function?</h1>
      <p class="byline">
        We are four final-year students in the Department of Computer Science and Engineering at the University of Moratuwa, supervised by {supervisor}.
      </p>
    </header>

    <article class="story prose">
      <section aria-labelledby="ch1">
        <h2 id="ch1">What we set out to do</h2>
        <p>
          Train a neural network on one task, then on a second, and it forgets most of the first. The new training rewrites the weights the old task
          depended on. One family of fixes sets aside part of the network for each task and freezes it, so later tasks cannot touch it. The best-known
          of these, PackNet, decides what to keep by weight size: large weights stay, small ones are handed to the next task.
        </p>
        <p>
          We wanted to choose differently: keep the parts that shape how the network behaves, even when their weights are small. A recently published
          method, Artificial Structure Function Search (ASF-S), offers a way to measure this. It watches how the network's output units respond together,
          and scores every convolutional filter by how much it shapes that pattern. Its paper reports that a small convolutional network on CIFAR-10
          keeps most of its accuracy after losing three quarters of its parameters, with only the last layer retrained. That was our starting point.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/research/">How the project fits together</a></p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="ch2">
        <h2 id="ch2">What we found when we checked ASF-S</h2>
        <p>
          Before building on ASF-S, we checked that it does what its paper says. It mostly does not.
        </p>
        <p>
          We <a href={link("cifar10-repro")}>rebuilt it from scratch on the paper's own setting</a> and checked every step against the paper.
          Every step up to the last matches. The last one does not: after pruning, we lose 10.50 points of accuracy where the paper reports 3.78.
          Scoring filters with the formula exactly as the paper writes it does no better than picking filters at random (−15.00 against −15.07).
          Retraining the whole network instead of the last layer brings every method to about −5, and then the choice of filters hardly matters.
        </p>
        <p>
          The other checks agree. On CIFAR-100, <a href={link("cifar100-port-audit")}>after we fixed three bugs in the ported code</a>, ASF-S kept
          40.46% accuracy against 52.52% unpruned, and its scores were barely better than random. On the paper's smallest experiment,
          <a href={link("mnist-lenet-sanity")}>LeNet-300-100 on MNIST</a>, the trained network reproduces exactly, but ASF-S never beats pruning
          the smallest weights. A <a href={link("bert-pg-transfer")}>test on a BERT language model</a> could not separate any method from any other.
        </p>
        <p>
          So we stopped reproducing and started measuring. We rebuilt the method as a <a href={link("s0-skeleton")}>tested package</a> that matches
          the original code exactly, and we are now <a href={link("s1-baselines")}>comparing our corrected score with eleven other ways of choosing filters</a>
          under one fair protocol. So far it beats random choice but loses to Taylor importance and to weight size.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/studies/">Every study, with its evidence</a></p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="ch3">
        <h2 id="ch3">What we are doing next</h2>
        <p>
          By 6 December we decide whether the functional score earns a place in our method. We wrote the test down before running it: the corrected
          score must come within the 95% confidence interval of the best simple score at the same size, or preserve the network's functional pattern
          measurably better. If it does neither, it becomes one option among several, and our contribution moves to two things that hold either way:
          deciding how much of the network each task gets, and the reproduction itself.
        </p>
        <p>
          From December we build the two continual learning methods our proposal set out. The first freezes each task's most important units. The
          second makes them harder to change without freezing them. In February we compare both against PackNet and other established methods on
          Split-CIFAR, over five seeds, and look at the test set once, at the very end.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/timeline/">The timeline</a></p>
      </section>
    </article>

    <nav class="story-foot" aria-label="More">
      <a href="/studies/">Studies</a>
      {hasNotes && <a href="/blog/">Lab notes</a>}
      <a href="/research/#records">How we keep records</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/NeuroPulse-Co">Code on GitHub</a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</Base>
